---
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import Icon from "../components/Icon.astro";
import Wordmark from "../components/Wordmark.astro";
import { APPLE, GITHUB, LINUX, WINDOWS } from "../lib/icons";

const repo = "https://github.com/mory-dev/sudonotes";
---

<Base
  title="Download sudonotes"
  description="How to get sudonotes today: build it from source in a couple of commands. Signed installers for Windows, macOS and Linux are being prepared."
>
  <section class="page prose" style="padding-block: clamp(44px, 7vw, 76px)">
    <p class="eyebrow">Download</p>
    <h1>Get sudonotes</h1>

    <div class="note warn">
      <strong>There are no prebuilt installers yet.</strong> <Wordmark /> is at v0.1.1 and the release
      pipeline — cross-platform builds, code signing and macOS notarization — is not finished.
      Rather than hand you an unsigned binary that your OS will warn you about, the honest answer
      today is: build it from source. It takes two commands and about a minute.
    </div>

    <h2>Build it yourself</h2>
    <p>
      You need <a href="https://nodejs.org">Node.js</a>, <a href="https://rustup.rs">Rust</a>, and
      the <a href="https://tauri.app/start/prerequisites/">Tauri system dependencies</a> for your
      platform.
    </p>

    <pre><code>git clone https://github.com/mory-dev/sudonotes.git</code></pre>
    <pre><code>cd sudonotes/app && npm install && npm run tauri build</code></pre>

    <p>
      The installer lands in <code>app/src-tauri/target/release/bundle/</code>. To run it in
      development instead, use <code>npm run tauri dev</code>.
    </p>

    <h2>What you get</h2>
    <ul>
      <li class="platform-line">
        <span class="platforms">
          <Icon path={WINDOWS} size={14} label="Windows" />
          <Icon path={APPLE} size={14} label="macOS" />
          <Icon path={LINUX} size={14} label="Linux" />
        </span>
        <span><strong>Windows, macOS and Linux</strong> — one codebase, native builds.</span>
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>About 20&nbsp;MB.</strong> Tauri uses your system's webview rather than shipping a
        browser.
      </li>
      <li><strong>No account.</strong> First launch asks you where to keep your vault. That is it.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>When installers land</h2>
    <p>
      Signed builds need an Apple Developer account for notarization and a Windows signing
      certificate; both cost money and take time to set up. Until they are in place, an unsigned
      installer would trip SmartScreen and Gatekeeper — which is a worse first impression than a
      clone and a build. Progress is on the <a href="/roadmap">roadmap</a>.
    </p>

    <div class="actions">
      <a class="button primary" href={repo}><Icon path={GITHUB} size={16} />Open the repository</a>
      <a class="button" href="/docs/vault-format">See what it writes to disk</a>
    </div>
  </section>
</Base>

<style>
  .platform-line {
    display: flex;
    align-items: baseline;
    gap: 9px;
  }

  .platforms {
    flex: none;
    display: inline-flex;
    align-items: center;
    gap: 6px;
    transform: translateY(2px);
  }
</style>
